t an action? But then no observation, so might be allowed? However typical ReAct requires action to gather info. But spec says \"Permit Final only at the end and only at a reasoning boundary.\" A reasoning boundary could be after a Thought without an action? That seems unlikely. Better to enforce: After Thought, must be Action unless it's the last line and it's Final? But then Thought then Final? That would be a short trajectory. But the example has Action after first Thought, then Observation, then second Thought, then Final. So the pattern: Thought, Action, Observation, ..., then Thought, Final. So after Thought you can have Action (if not final) or Final (if end). But if after Thought you have Final, it's allowed as a final step. However after Observation you cannot have Final directly? The spec says \"Permit Final only at the end and only at a reasoning boundary.\" After Observation is a reasoning boundary? Possibly yes. But then why the example has an extra Thought before Final? Because they wanted to have a final thought before answer. So we need to decide.\n\nLet's interpret: The ReAct pattern: Thought -> Action -> Observation -> (Thought/stop). So after Observation, you can have a new Thought or stop with Final? Final is \"stop\". So after Observation, you can have Final. But the example shows an extra Thought. That suggests Final can come after Thought (as a separate reasoning step) or after Observation. To be safe, we should allow Final after either Thought or Observation, but only at the end.\n\nBut the spec says \"Require Thought then Action then Observation for each nonterminal cycle.\" So each nonterminal cycle is T-A-O. Then after that, either another T-A-O or stop with Final. The Final can be after a Thought? Or after an Observation? The wording \"only at the end and only at a reasoning boundary\" - reasoning boundary could be after an Observation (since that's when new info is received) or after a Thought (since that's when you decide to stop). I think we can allow Final after either Thought or Observation, but must be last. However to match example, they placed Final after a Thought. So likely rule: After Observation, you must have either Thought or Final. After Thought, you must have either Action or Final. So the state machine transitions:\n\nStates: START, AFTER_THOUGHT, AFTER_ACTION, AFTER_OBSERVATION.\n\n- START: expect Thought.\n- AFTER_THOUGHT: expect Action or Final (if end). If Final, end.\n- AFTER_ACTION: expect Observation.\n- AFTER_OBSERVATION: expect Thought or Final (if end).\n\nIf any other line type occurs, it's a violation.\n\nAlso, Final must appear only at end. So if we encounter Final and there are more lines after, violation.\n\nNeed to validate each line format: type: content. Type must be one of Thought, Action, Observation, Final (case-insensitive? Possibly exact as given? The task says \"Parse and validate interleaved Thought, Action, Action Input, and Observation records\". But Action Input is mentioned? Actually \"Action Input\" is separate? In the fixture, there is no Action Input. The lines have only Action. The spec mentions \"Action, Action Input\" but maybe Action Input is optional? The fixture doesn't have it. So we probably only need to handle these four types. The lines start with type then colon then content.\n\nWe need to parse content after colon. Preserve content as string.\n\nReturn records with lowercase types: \"thought\", \"action\", \"observation\", \"final\". And content.\n\nValidity flag: \"valid\" boolean.\n\nViolation: if invalid, return \"violation\" object with \"line\" (one-based index) and \"prefix\" (list of records before violation). The prefix should be the valid records up to the line before the violation. If violation on first line, prefix is empty list.\n\nIf valid, violation is null or not present? The spec says \"On the first violation return its one-based line and the valid prefix.\" So if valid, we don't include violation. We should have \"valid\":