content}\n        records.append(record)\n        \n        # update state\n        if type_expected == 'Thought':\n            state = 'AFTER_THOUGHT'\n        elif type_expected == 'Action':\n            state = 'AFTER_ACTION'\n        elif type_expected == 'Action Input':\n            state = 'AFTER_ACTION_INPUT'\n        elif type_expected == 'Observation':\n            state = 'AFTER_OBSERVATION'\n        elif type_expected == 'Final':\n            state = 'END'  # but we break loop anyway\n        \n        if valid is False:\n            break\n    \n    # After loop, if valid and state not 'END'? Actually if loop completed without violation, check if last record is Final\n    if valid and (not records or records[-1]['type'] != 'final'):\n        # violation: last line is not Final\n        valid = False\n        # the violation is at the last line? But we already processed all lines. So we need to set violation on the last line? But we didn't detect it earlier. Let's handle: if last line was not Final, it's a violation at that line. So we should have broken on that line. But we didn't because we only check after processing? We need to restructure.\n        # Better to check after each line: if after processing line, if state is not 'END' and it's the last line? Or better: after loop, if valid and state != 'END', then violation at last line (since it should have been Final). But we need prefix up to last line (excluding last line). So we can set violation at index = len(lines) (last line) with prefix = records[:-1].\n        # However we already added the last record to records. So we can set violation line = len(lines), prefix = records[:-1].\n        line_num = len(lines)\n        violation = {'line': line_num, 'prefix': records[:-1]}\n        # remove the last record from records? The spec says \"valid prefix\" is records before violation. So final output should have records only up to before violation? Or the whole set? The spec says \"Return lowercase typed trajectory records and an explicit validity flag. On the first violation return its one-based line and the valid prefix.\" It doesn't specify that the records should be truncated to prefix. Usually you return the prefix as valid records, and maybe the full records until violation? But to be safe, we should set records to the prefix? The spec says \"valid prefix\" meaning the part that is valid before violation. So in the output, the records should be the prefix (the valid ones). The violation line is the line where violation occurred. So we should output records = prefix (the valid ones before violation). So if violation at last line, prefix = records without last record. So we set records = prefix. Also set valid = False.\n        records = violation['prefix']\n\n    elif valid:\n        # all good\n        pass\n    \n    result = {'valid': valid, 'records': records}\n    if violation is not None:\n        result['violation'] = violation\n    \n    return result\n\nBut note: In the loop, we already break on first violation and set prefix = records[:] which is the records before the current line. That's correct. For the case of missing Final at end, we handle after loop.\n\nWe also need to consider scenario where there is no line at all? Then valid? Possibly invalid because no Final? We'll handle: if lines empty, then no records, no Final, violation at line 0? Not defined. Probably not a case. We'll assume at least one line.\n\nTesting with example fixture: lines: T, A, O, T, F. Should be valid. Our state machine: START -> Thought -> AFTER_THOUGHT -> Action -> AFTER_ACTION -> Observation (allowed? After Action, allowed types: Action Input, Observation. Observation is allowed.) -> AFTER_OBSERVATION -> Thought -> AFTER_THOUGHT -> Final (allowed) and line is last, so Final passes. Loop ends, state is 'END' (set to 'END' but we didn't define 'END' in expected_types, but we don't need it). After loop, valid=True, records[-1]['type']=='final', so no violation. Good.\n\nWhat about invalid order: e.g., T, A, T? The second T after Action not allowed because AFTER_ACTION expects Action Input or Observation. So violation at line 3 (index 3) with prefix = first two records. Works.\n\nWhat about missing Final at end: T, A, O. After loop, records[-1] is Observation, not Final. valid=False. Violation line=3, prefix=records[:2] (T and A). Records set to prefix. Output valid=False, records=[T,A], violation line=3 prefix=[T,A]. But note: the violation is on line 3 (Observation) which is indeed not Final. That seems correct.\n\nEdge: If only Final? That would be state START -> Final. Is Final allowed at start? expected_types['START'] includes only Thought. So violation. So only Final alone is invalid. Good.\n\nNow, we need to output as JSON-serializable. The function returns dict.\n\nWe must ensure we don't mutate case. We don't.\n\nNow produce final answer: exactly one JSON object with key \"implementation\" containing Python source code as JSON string. Must escape newlines, backslashes, quotes. We'll write the code as a string.\n\nWe need to wrap in triple quotes for Python string? No, we need to put the whole source as a JSON string value. So we need to properly escape.\n\nWe'll construct the Python code as a multiline string, then use json.dumps to escape it. But since we are writing the JSON manually, we need to escape. Simpler: write the code with proper escaping: replace backslash with double backslash, newline with \\n, double quote with \\\".\n\nLet's write the code in a clean manner.\n\nWe'll define solve function. Ensure no imports beyond standard library. Use type hints optional.\n\nNow produce final JSON.\n\nNote: The task says \"Return one JSON object with exactly one key, `implementation`.\" So we output {\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    ...\"}.\n\nMake sure no extra text.\n\nLet's write the code."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":690,"completion_tokens":5303,"total_tokens":5993,"